PLACES=[
N('fort-plain-museum','Fort Plain Museum and the fort hill','Fort Plain','mohawk','fort','traces',42.94016,-74.6302,'site','open','walk','forts','1776–1786',
  'On this hill above the Mohawk and Otsquago Creek stood Fort Plain, a Revolutionary War post. Wikipedia gives the fort as built in 1776 with a blockhouse garrisoned through the war; the Journal of the American Revolution has the large fort built in 1781, replacing smaller posts; the state marker on Canal Street gives 1776–1786 and says the August 1780 raid by Brant\'s Indians and Tories reached no farther. The museum is in the Greek Revival farmhouse of the Lipe family on the hill.',
  'Few traces of the fort survive, per the Journal of the American Revolution (2025): a walking tour leads to the excavated site of a blockhouse, the spring that watered the garrison, and remnants of trenches and earthworks, on grounds open for walking and picnics. Inside the house are artifacts from the site, among them the remains of a tin box of priming quills for the fort\'s cannon. FORT PLAIN is spelled out on the hillside facing the Thruway.',
  'The hill a Revolutionary fort was built on, with its blockhouse dug out and its spring still there.',
  'Fort Plain Museum and Historical Park, 389 Canal Street (NY 5S), on the west edge of the village; phone (518) 993-2527. The hours are from the museum\'s own contact page, read November 20, 2026; groups by arrangement. The Town of Minden\'s page gives older, seasonal hours (Wednesday to Sunday, May to September), so call out of season. Parking at the museum not checked.',
  [L('Fort Plain Museum: contact and hours','https://fortplainmuseum.com/contact'),L('Journal of the American Revolution: Profile of the Fort Plain Museum (2025)','https://allthingsliberty.com/2025/04/profile-fort-plain-museum-and-historical-park/'),L('Town of Minden: Fort Plain Museum','https://www.townofmindenny.gov/?p=50'),W('Fort Plain, New York'),HM(5286,'Fort Plain')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Friday to Monday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('otsquago-creek-aqueduct-fort-plain','Otsquago Creek aqueduct walls','Fort Plain','mohawk','canal','ruins',42.93043,-74.62133,'site','public','walk','canal','1847–1848',
  'The enlarged Erie Canal crossed Otsquago Creek in the middle of Fort Plain on a stone and timber aqueduct built about 1847 and 1848. The canal was moved to the Mohawk River in 1916 and the aqueduct\'s stonework was used to carry a highway bridge, per an HMdb canal panel; a Pomeroy Foundation marker of 2021 says a flood destroyed it in 1981.',
  'Remnants of the aqueduct\'s walls beside the creek at East State Street, where the Pomeroy marker stands; the canal panel says only the abutments survive after years of floods. Condition on the ground not checked.',
  'An Erie Canal aqueduct that became a road bridge and then a ruin.',
  'Village street at 1 East State Street, by the creek: the remnants are seen from the public street and the marker there; who owns the creek bank was not checked. Parking at Haslett Park on Legion Street, about 130 m northwest, which the federal land map shows as a park.',
  [HM(286212,'Canal Aqueduct (Pomeroy Foundation, 2021)'),HM(253539,'Erie Canal in Fort Plain')],th=[42.93125,-74.62251],thn='Haslett Park lot, Legion Street',brick='no'),
N('palatine-church','Palatine Church','Palatine','mohawk','worship','standing',42.96889,-74.62889,'site','public','drive','','1770',
  'Lutheran Palatine Germans built this small church in 1770 of limestone taken from the creek nearby, per the Mohawk Valley museums association; the Wagner, Nellis, Reber and Hess families paid for it and six Nellises for the spire and its gilded rooster. In Sir John Johnson\'s raid of October 1780, the farms along the road were burned; the story is that a British officer stopped his Indian allies from burning the church because he had promised his friend Henry Nellis to spare it. The museums association\'s page for it describes the Palatine Society, formed in 1938 to keep the memory of the Palatine settlers.',
  'A one-storey rectangle with massive stone walls and a meetinghouse plan (Wikipedia), on the Mohawk Turnpike (NY 5) east of Nelliston. The association\'s page says the grounds are open dawn to dusk. Interior not checked; no page read gives a service or opening date.',
  'The church a promise to a friend kept from the torch in 1780.',
  'Shown with the Palatine Society of the Evangelical Lutheran Church; who owns it not checked. Grounds open dawn to dusk per mohawkvalleymuseums.us (read November 20, 2026). On NY 5; no mapped lot, parking not checked.',
  [W('Palatine Church'),L('Mohawk Valley Museums: The Old Palatine Church','https://mohawkvalleymuseums.us/museums/the-old-palatine-church/'),L('Palatines to America: Old Palatine Church','https://palam.org/old-palatine-church'),HM(137547,'Palatine Evangelical Lutheran Church')],
  brick='no',hours='Grounds dawn to dusk'),
N('stone-arabia-reformed-church','Reformed Dutch Church of Stone Arabia','Stone Arabia','mohawk','worship','standing',42.94271,-74.55625,'exact','view','drive','','1788',
  'The congregation was organized in 1711, per the marker beside it. The first church on the shared glebe lot was burned with the rest of Stone Arabia in the battle and raid of October 19, 1780; this one of cut limestone was built in 1788. The marker says Reformed services were held here until 1990, and the county historian wrote in 2008 that of the town\'s three colonial congregations only Trinity Lutheran next door was still active.',
  'A plain rectangle of cut limestone blocks with a flattened gable roof, a belfry and a Georgian Palladian window (Wikipedia). Trinity Lutheran stands immediately south. Interior not checked; the county tourism page gives a phone for the churches and says to call to confirm hours.',
  'A limestone church raised on the ashes of 1780.',
  'Owner not checked; the county tourism listing names a society president to call, (518) 774-1998. On NY 10 in the hamlet; see it from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Reformed Dutch Church of Stone Arabia'),HM(136701,'Former Dutch Reformed Church'),CO('The Trinity Lutheran Church (2008)','articles_TrinityLutheranChurch.asp'),VM('Stone Arabia Churches','stone-arabia-churches')],
  brick='no'),
N('trinity-lutheran-stone-arabia','Trinity Lutheran Church and churchyard, Stone Arabia','Stone Arabia','mohawk','worship','standing',42.94158,-74.55585,'exact','view','drive','','1792',
  'Palatine settlers bought the glebe lot in 1729 and built a log church; the Lutherans and the Reformed split and divided the lot in 1744 along the brook. Sir John Johnson\'s troops burned the log church in the Battle of Stone Arabia on October 19, 1780. The present frame church was begun in 1790 and dedicated on July 1, 1792; the congregation is one of the few Lutheran ones in America to have worshipped on one site for almost three centuries (county historian, 2008).',
  'A two-storey frame church facing east, Federal with Italianate changes, and a chapel (1892–95) and hall (1923–31) on the west end. Inside, per the county historian: a centre aisle, the dates 1791 and 1882 carved over the door into the nave, and a gallery over the narthex that was once the choir loft. About 25 stones stand in the fenced churchyard to the west, the earliest of 1752. Service times not found.',
  'A congregation on the same ground since 1729, in its rebuilt church of 1792.',
  'An active church, 5430 NY 10; see it and the churchyard from the road unless at a service. No page of the congregation\'s own was found, so no service times. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Trinity Lutheran Church and Cemetery (Stone Arabia, New York)'),CO('The Trinity Lutheran Church (2008)','articles_TrinityLutheranChurch.asp'),VM('Stone Arabia Churches','stone-arabia-churches')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('van-alstyne-homestead','Van Alstyne Homestead','Canajoharie','mohawk','landmark','standing',42.90361,-74.57224,'exact','open','walk','','before 1730',
  'A fieldstone Dutch house built by Martin Janse Van Alstyne before 1730, later also a tavern. By 1775 Goshen Van Alstyne owned it, and it was a frequent meeting place of the Tryon County Committee of Safety; Wikipedia says Nicholas Herkimer accepted his commission here. The artist Rufus Grider bought and renovated it in 1885 as a museum. Its old name Fort Rensselaer is a misnomer: Wikipedia\'s Canajoharie article says the actual fort was at Fort Plain.',
  'A long, low stone house with a steep gambrel roof and a garret, and a frame addition across the back. The Van Alstyne Homestead Society shows it with colonial furnishings and Rufus Grider\'s drawings of 18th-century Mohawk Valley houses and farms.',
  'Where the Mohawk Valley\'s patriots met before the Revolution.',
  'Van Alstyne Homestead Society, 42 Moyer Street; phone (315) 269-2198. The county tourism listing gives Saturdays noon to 4 p.m., May 23 to September 5, but carries 2015 events and is a directory, not the society\'s own page: hours not checked. Street parking in the village; lots on Mohawk and East Main Streets about 300 m away.',
  [W('Van Alstyne Homestead'),VM('Van Alstyne Homestead Society and Museum','van-alstyne-homestead-society-and-museum')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('arkell-museum-canajoharie','Arkell Museum and Canajoharie Library','Canajoharie','mohawk','civic','standing',42.90759,-74.57223,'exact','open','walk','','1924–1927',
  'Bartlett Arkell, the leading industrialist of Canajoharie and head of Beech-Nut, founded the library in 1924 and added a gallery in 1927, at first to show copies of European masterpieces and then original American paintings he bought and gave. Wikipedia lists rooms modelled on galleries he admired at the Metropolitan Museum, the Walker Art Gallery in Liverpool and the Rijksmuseum. Beech-Nut left the village for a new plant in the town of Florida in 2011.',
  'American paintings mostly of 1860 to 1940, and exhibits on the Mohawk Valley and Beech-Nut. Building fabric not checked.',
  'A first-rate American art collection in a village of 2,000 off the Thruway.',
  'Run by the Arkell Museum; its own site (arkellmuseum.org) refused every connection on November 20, 2026, so hours and fees not checked. Village street parking.',
  [W('Arkell Museum'),W('Canajoharie, New York')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('canajoharie-historic-district','Canajoharie village and its historic district','Canajoharie','mohawk','town','standing',42.90306,-74.57111,'area','public','walk','','about 1750–1959',
  'Listed in 2015, the district takes in 836 buildings of the business district and the streets round it, built from about 1750 to 1959. Charles Shaper opened the town\'s first limestone quarry in 1849; HMdb\'s canal panels say local stone went into Erie Canal locks, the New York Central and the abutments of the Brooklyn Bridge, and into many stone buildings in the village. Beech-Nut, founded here in 1890, built its plants beside the canal; they are named in the district listing, but the company left in 2011 and WAMC reported in April 2025 that the plant had been demolished with a state grant and the site was a flattened, vacant lot.',
  'Per Wikipedia: the 1842 Reformed church, Good Shepherd Episcopal (1874), St. Mark\'s Lutheran (1868), Saints Peter and Paul (1862 and 1890s), the Sayles Building (1868), the 1941 village hall, the Arkell complex, and the stone West Hill School of 1891–93 with its bell tower, built on the site of the academy where Susan B. Anthony taught in 1846. The Shaper Quarry marker is at Cliff Street and Shaper Avenue.',
  'A limestone village that sent its stone to the Brooklyn Bridge.',
  'Public village streets (Village of Canajoharie); private buildings seen from the sidewalk. Lots on East Main and Mohawk Streets (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Canajoharie Historic District'),W('West Hill School (New York)'),HM(130956,'Shaper Quarry'),L('WAMC (April 2025): Canajoharie still waiting for clarity on future of former Beech-Nut site','https://www.wamc.org/news/2025-04-21/canajoharie-still-waiting-for-clarity-on-future-of-former-beech-nut-site')],
  brick='?'),
N('frey-house-palatine-bridge','Frey House and Fort Frey','Palatine Bridge','mohawk','landmark','standing',42.91194,-74.58444,'site','view','drive','forts','1808',
  'Hendrick Frey settled at the foot of the hill here in 1689, and the state marker puts a British fort nearby in 1701–1713 and calls this the home of Major John Frey. The present house was built in 1808, with a stone kitchen wing added in 1882 and a sun porch in 1931 (Wikipedia).',
  'A double-pile, centre-hall house; on the property, per Wikipedia, are a garage of about 1930, a 19th-century lime kiln and the Frey family cemetery. Private: interior not checked, and whether the kiln can be seen from the road is not checked.',
  'A house with its own lime kiln on ground settled in 1689.',
  'Private home on West Grand Street (NY 5); look from the road. The Fort Frey marker is on NY 5.',
  [W('Frey House'),HM(137581,'Fort Frey')],
  brick='?'),
N('montgomery-county-old-courthouse-fonda','Old Montgomery County Courthouse','Fonda','mohawk','civic','standing',42.95378,-74.37585,'site','open','walk','','1836',
  'Built in 1836 when the county seat moved from Johnstown to Fonda, per the state marker in front, and the scene of many social, religious and political meetings. It now holds the county\'s Department of History and Archives, which calls it one of the largest government-owned collections of genealogical records.',
  'The courthouse faces the railroad on Park Street, with the marker by the flagpole. Visitors come in to the archives on weekdays. Building fabric not checked.',
  'An 1836 courthouse that keeps the county\'s memory.',
  'Montgomery County Department of History and Archives, Old Court House, 9 Park Street; phone (518) 853-8186. Hours from the county\'s contact page, read November 20, 2026; research appointments can be booked there. Street parking in Fonda.',
  [CO('Location and hours','contact.asp'),HM(59070,'Court House')],
  brick='?',hours='Monday to Friday 8:30 a.m. to 4 p.m. (July and August 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.)'),
N('auriesville-shrine-coliseum','Coliseum at the Shrine of Our Lady of Martyrs','Auriesville','mohawk','worship','standing',42.92552,-74.30199,'exact','open','drive','','1930',
  'A shrine on the hill above the Mohawk honours the Jesuits Isaac Jogues, René Goupil and Jean de Lalande, killed at the Mohawk village of Ossernenon in 1642 and 1646, and Kateri Tekakwitha, born there in 1656. The first church was built in 1884 and the Jesuits ran the shrine for over 130 years until they gave the deed to a lay group in 2017; the bishops made it a national shrine in 2025. Wikipedia notes that archaeologists put Ossernenon itself about 9 miles to the west.',
  'The Coliseum, one of the first round churches in the country: a huge circular building with plain wooden benches round a high altar built to look like a log stockade (OSV News, 2025). Wikipedia says it was built in 1930 and seats about 6,000; the HMdb marker gives 1930, OSV News says completed in 1931 and holding nearly 10,000. A small museum and a visitor centre stand on the grounds.',
  'A church built round like an arena, for pilgrim crowds of thousands.',
  'Run by the shrine (ourladyofmartyrsshrine.org). No parking or admission fee; dogs on leashes on the grounds only. Its calendar (read November 20, 2026) shows the season closed from October 20, 2026, reopening May 1, 2027; its FAQ gives the grounds 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. daily in season and the museum Friday to Monday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. Coliseum Masses in 2026 were on the calendar (Sundays 3 p.m.); check it before going. Lot on Shrine Road east of the Coliseum.',
  [L('Our Lady of Martyrs Shrine: FAQ (hours, fees)','https://ourladyofmartyrsshrine.org/faq'),L('Our Lady of Martyrs Shrine: calendar','https://www.ourladyofmartyrsshrine.org/calendar'),W('National Shrine of the North American Martyrs'),L('OSV News (2025): Visiting update on the National Shrine of Our Lady of Martyrs','https://trentonmonitor.com/news/2025/jun/02/visiting-update-on-new-yorks-national-shrine-of-our-lady-of-martyrs'),HM(70846,'The National Martyrs\' Shrine of America')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.92558,-74.30053],thn='Shrine lot on Shrine Road',brick='?',hours='May 1 to about October 19: grounds daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; museum Friday to Monday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. Closed for the season until May 1, 2027.'),
N('fultonville-historic-district','Fultonville, the canal village','Fultonville','mohawk','town','standing',42.94792,-74.36625,'area','public','walk','canal','about 1824–',
  'Fultonville was laid out about 1824, as the Erie Canal neared, and was a busy canal port; it was incorporated in 1848 and named for Robert Fulton. John H. Starin, born nearby, made his fortune in New York shipping and ferries and came home in 1874, building an estate on the hill and giving the village a silk mill, the 1883 Fultonville National Bank, a library and a hall. Close to 40 buildings were moved or torn down in the early 1950s for the Thruway, which runs through the old business section (county historian, 2006). The rest became the Fultonville Historic District in 2019.',
  'Main Street and the streets off it, with houses of the canal era; the county historian names the Reformed church of 1856, the Methodist church on Montgomery Street (organized 1854), the old Calvary Baptist church on Main Street, and the park with a marker where the 1884 Union Free School stood. Which buildings stand was not checked on the ground.',
  'A canal port cut in half by the Thruway, with its old village still beside it.',
  'Public village streets (Village of Fultonville); buildings private unless marked. Street parking.',
  [W('Fultonville, New York'),CO('Fultonville History (2006)','articles_FultonvilleHistory.asp'),HM(131480,'Fultonville Union Free High School')],
  brick='?'),
N('glen-historic-district','Glen hamlet','Glen','mohawk','town','standing',42.89444,-74.3425,'area','public','drive','','',
  'A rural crossroads hamlet listed as a historic district in 2001: 52 buildings, most of them one-and-a-half or two-storey timber frames with gable roofs. The county historian says the hamlet, once called Voorheesville, was ahead of Fultonville until the canal came.',
  'At the crossroads, per Wikipedia: two large Federal houses, a general store of the mid-19th century and a Second Empire brick house of 1878. All private; see them from the road.',
  'A crossroads hamlet the canal passed by.',
  'Public town roads at the crossroads; the houses are private, so look from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Glen Historic District'),CO('Fultonville History (2006)','articles_FultonvilleHistory.asp')],
  brick='some'),
N('nelliston-historic-district','Nelliston\'s streets and railroad station','Nelliston','mohawk','town','standing',42.93389,-74.61667,'area','public','walk','stations','1860–1902',
  'Three residential streets of Nelliston built between 1860 and 1890, with the ground along the railroad holding two freight houses and the 1902 station, listed together in 1980 (Wikipedia).',
  'The Abram Nellis mansion, a brick Italianate house of the 1860s, the houses of the 1860s to 1890s, and the station and freight houses by the track. Their present state not checked; the railroad is in use, so keep off the tracks.',
  'A railroad village of the 1880s with its station still by the track.',
  'Public village streets (Village of Nelliston); private houses, so look from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Nelliston Historic District')],
  brick='some'),
N('ames-academy','Ames Academy','Ames','mohawk','civic','standing',42.83694,-74.6,'site','view','drive','','1835',
  'A school built in 1835 of cut limestone, chartered by the Regents as the Ames Academy in 1839 and used as a school until 1959. Since 1987 it has held a local history museum (Wikipedia).',
  'A two-storey stone building, five bays by two, with a gable roof. Museum hours not found; interior not checked.',
  'An 1835 stone academy turned village museum.',
  'Who runs the museum and when it opens not found; see it from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Ames Academy Building')],
  brick='no'),
N('first-baptist-church-charleston','First Baptist Church, Charleston','Charleston','mohawk','worship','standing',42.84861,-74.34528,'site','view','drive','cemeteries','1820s',
  'A country church on Polin Road, believed built in the 1820s and remodelled in the 1860s, for a congregation organized about 1793. The Charleston Historical Society bought it in 1978 (Wikipedia).',
  'A timber-framed church of late Federal and Greek Revival style with a square, hip-roofed bell tower; beside it a cemetery mostly of the early 19th century, a dry-laid stone wall and a receiving vault. Interior not checked.',
  'A hill-town Baptist church and its burying ground, kept by the local historical society.',
  'Charleston Historical Society, owner since 1978 per Wikipedia; when it is opened not found. See it and the cemetery from Polin Road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('First Baptist Church (Charleston, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
]
